      "BlockNode:00CDH8S908005",
      "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G000",
      "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Verb → module**",
    "text": "**Verb → module**: a static table in the new `packages/cli/src/aperas.ts` (verb string -> the matching `kgX.ts` module), not filesystem auto-discovery — there are ~19 fixed commands, a static map is simpler and makes an unrecognized verb a clean, listable error rather than \"whatever happens to be on disk.\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDH8S908003",
    "text": "**The real gap: every `kgX.ts`'s `main()` is private.** Each file already separates a pure `run*` function from its own `main()` (argv-parsing, stdin, printing) — but `main()` itself is never exported, only invoked at the bottom of the file behind `if (process.argv[1]?.endsWith('kgX.ts')) main()...`. A dispatcher importing the module and expecting to call its `main()` needs that export added, in every one of the ~19 files. The self-invocation guard stays exactly as-is underneath that export — direct `tsx kgX.ts` (what the existing `kg:*` npm scripts still do) keeps working unchanged; the dispatcher is a second caller of the same exported function, not a replacement for the first.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDH8S908004",
    "text": "**argv re-slicing.** `main()` reads `process.argv.slice(2)` directly (not a parameter) expecting its own args to start there. Invoked as `aperas <verb> <rest...>`, `process.argv` is `[node, aperas.ts, <verb>, ...rest]` — one extra element ahead of what any `main()` expects. The dispatcher has to splice the verb back out of `process.argv` itself before calling the target's `main()`, since nothing here can change what `main()` reads from.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDH8S908005",
    "text": "**No verb / unrecognized verb / `--help`.** The static verb table doubles as the source for a top-level listing (verb + one-line description, authored once in `aperas.ts`, not scraped from each command's own `kgHelp.ts` output — that's only reachable by actually invoking a specific command's `--help`, not introspectable ahead of time without doing so). `aperas <verb> --help` forwards straight into that verb's own existing `main()`, which already implements it via `wantsHelp`/`printHelp` — nothing new needed there.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G000:links:Link:00CDH8S9Q8002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDDVC33G009",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G000:links:Link:00CDH8S9Q8002:props:StringProp:00CDH8S9QG000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "298"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G000:links:Link:00CDH8S9QG001",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDDVC8N0002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G000:links:Link:00CDH8S9QG001:props:StringProp:00CDH8S9QG002",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "443"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G000:links:Link:00CDH8S9QG003",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDDVCDW8002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G000:links:Link:00CDH8S9QG003:props:StringProp:00CDH8S9QG004",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "641"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDH8S90G000",
    "text": "**Entry point, deliberately punted on the bigger question.** A root `\"aperas\": \"tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts\"` script (`npm run aperas -- <verb> <args>`) is enough to make the dispatcher real and testable today, same mechanism every `kg:*` script already uses. This deliberately does *not* touch [the runtime question](#id/BlockNode:00CDDVC33G009) (a real compiled build + `bin` field + shebang, for an actual installable global command) or [the graph-location question](../issues/packaging.md#id/BlockNode:00CDDVC8N0002) — both stay open, tracked separately; the dispatcher mechanism itself doesn't depend on either being answered first ([Implementation Plan](../planning/packaging.md#id/BlockNode:00CDDVCDW8002) already says as much).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8S90G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Verification**",
    "text": "**Verification**: spot-check a handful of verbs through `aperas <verb>` against their `kg:<verb>` equivalent (same output), and re-run the full `npm run verify` harness — it calls `run*` functions directly, so the dispatcher layer shouldn't be able to affect it either way; a regression there would mean something broke in a shared file, not the dispatcher itself.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDH8S908001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDH8WQV8001",
