        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T12:40:49.166Z",
    "title": "00CDHFMMKR001",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDDVC8N0006",
    "type": "list",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4R8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Freeflow: why the runtime question isn't just \"run tsc\"",
    "text": "Asked to explain why this stays open rather than being a trivial build step. Recorded here since it bundles a few real sub-decisions, not one.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CDDTBQWG000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG000",
      "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG001",
      "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG004",
      "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG005",
      "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG006"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The immediate problem**",
    "text": "**The immediate problem**: every `kgX.ts`/`aperas.ts` is executed via `tsx` — an on-the-fly TypeScript-to-JS transpiler, listed as a `devDependency`. That's fine for dogfooding (`npm run aperas -- tree` inside this checkout, `tsx` already installed as part of `npm install`), but a published package's `bin` entry has to be something Node can execute directly on an end user's machine, with nothing but the shebang line to go on (`#!/usr/bin/env node`) — raw `.ts` isn't that, on the range of Node versions a published package would need to support.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4R8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDHFW4RG001",
    "text": "**At least two genuinely different shapes this could take, not yet chosen between**:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4R8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG002",
      "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG002:props:StringProp:00CE598TS0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG002:props:StringProp:00CE598TS8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDHFW4RG002",
    "text": "Compile to plain JS at publish time (`tsc`/`esbuild`), ship the output, `bin` points at a `#!/usr/bin/env node`-shebanged compiled entrypoint. No runtime TS dependency for the end user, but now there's a real build step and a decision about *how* to compile (see below).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDHFW4RG003",
    "text": "Ship the `.ts` source as-is and make `tsx` (or an equivalent) a real dependency rather than a dev one, with the `bin` shebang invoking it directly. No build step, but every invocation pays transpilation cost, and it's an unusual shape for a published CLI (most don't ship raw TS to end users).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The concrete complication that makes \"just bundle it\" non-trivial**",
    "text": "**The concrete complication that makes \"just bundle it\" non-trivial**: `oxigraph` — the graph store this whole engine is built on — ships as a WASM binary (`node_bg.wasm`) plus a thin JS wrapper, not plain JS. A single-file bundle (esbuild et al.) can't just inline that the way it would inline ordinary TS/JS; it either needs to be left external (`--external:oxigraph`, meaning the published package still needs real `node_modules` dependency resolution, not a single self-contained script) or the wasm asset needs to be copied alongside the bundle output and referenced correctly. Either way, \"compile everything into one file\" isn't the free win it would be for a pure-JS/TS codebase.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4R8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**One more thing a build step forces a decision on**",
    "text": "**One more thing a build step forces a decision on**: `packages/core`'s (and `cli`'s) own `exports` map today points straight at `.ts` source (`\"./*\": \"./src/*.ts\"`) — correct for the dev workspace, where every package is a sibling symlink and `tsx` handles the extension. A published package needs its own answer for what those exports point at instead (compiled `dist/*.js`, most likely) — and whether that's a *second*, publish-only exports shape layered on top of the dev one (conditional exports keyed by `\"import\"`/`\"development\"` or similar), or a wholesale switch, is itself an open call.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4R8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4RG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDHFW4RG006",
    "text": "None of this is decided yet — flagged as open specifically because answering it means picking a compiler, a bundling strategy that accounts for `oxigraph`'s WASM binary, and how the workspace's own `exports` maps adapt for a built artifact, not just running one command.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHFW4R8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHJGZV0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Settled",
    "text": "Settled: `esbuild` (not `tsc`) bundling `packages/core`+`packages/cli` into one output file, `oxigraph` marked `--external` (it's WASM-backed, can't be inlined, and staying external is normal for a published package). The dev workspace's own `exports` maps (pointing at `.ts` source) stay exactly as they are — a separate build step produces its own `dist/`+minimal package.json for publishing, rather than one config trying to serve both dev and published via conditional exports. Trade-off accepted: a second thing to maintain (a build config), in exchange for the already-verified dev loop never having to change for a publish pipeline that doesn't exist yet.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CDDTBQWG000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK8R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Settled: graph discovery via two separate config files, not env-var-only",
    "text": "Started from a concrete pain point: `APERAS_MACHINE_NUMBER` (the snowflake id generator's machine-uniqueness input) is env-var-only today, left unset because there was nowhere better to put it, and warns on every single invocation as a result. That, plus a much bigger piece of context — the graph this all points at won't stay a single flat corpus. It's meant to grow into a tree: a corp graph, team graphs under it, member graphs under those, each mergeable up into its parent, the same shape a member's private notes eventually get merged into a team's, and a team's into the corp's. A bare \"walk up from cwd looking for an `AperasKG/` directory\" (the plain-search idea floated earlier) only ever finds *one* graph — it has no way to express \"this graph's parent is that one,\" so it doesn't fit this shape at all. A real config file does.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CDDTBQWG000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90000",
      "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90003",
      "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDHSCK90000",
    "text": "**Two separate files, not one, because they're different *kinds* of setting with different lifecycles:**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK8R001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90001",
      "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90001:props:StringProp:00CE598T6R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90001:props:StringProp:00CE598T6R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDHSCK90001",
    "text": "**`identity.json`** — machine-local, never committed (gitignored, or living outside the repo entirely, e.g. `~/.config/aperas/identity.json`). Holds `APERAS_MACHINE_NUMBER`'s replacement: a `machineNumber` that must differ across machines by construction. Putting it in a *shared* config would be actively wrong, not just untidy — every clone/checkout would inherit the same committed number and start colliding on generated ids, silently, which is the worst way for a uniqueness mechanism to fail. Solving this also directly kills the \"complains every time\" annoyance: set once per machine instead of once per shell session.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDHSCK90002",
    "text": "**`aperas.config.json`** — shared, committed, describes a graph's own place in the corp/team/member tree. Kept deliberately minimal for now rather than designing the merge mechanics today (a separate, bigger question): just two pointers, `graph` (this level's own artifacts root) and `parent` (the graph above it to eventually merge into, absent for a root graph). Enough to express the hierarchy and be discoverable, without committing yet to *how* a merge actually walks it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDHSCK90003",
    "text": "Both replace the current `getArtifactsDir()`'s `__dirname`-relative hop-counting, which only ever made sense for \"the CLI's own source lives a fixed number of directories below the one graph it drives\" — never true for an installed CLI, and now also not expressive enough for a tree of graphs regardless of install-vs-source-checkout.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK8R001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK90004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Not yet decided (separate, later passes)",
    "text": "Not yet decided (separate, later passes): the actual merge mechanics (what \"member graph merges into team graph\" does at the node/id level), and how upward discovery behaves when *no* `aperas.config.json` is found at all (this repo's own dev setup should probably keep working via some fallback, likely the existing hardcoded-relative-path behavior, so the split docs/graph work already done doesn't need its own migration just to keep functioning).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK8R001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQE8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Configuration",
    "text": "Two separate config files, not one — different lifecycles, never mixed:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDDVBXH8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG000",
      "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG001",
      "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG002",
      "BlockNode:00CDXSVBNG001",
      "BlockNode:00CDXSVBNR002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG000:props:StringProp:00CE598S5G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG000:props:StringProp:00CE598S5G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDHSJQEG000",
    "text": "`identity.json` — machine-local, never committed. Lives at `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/aperas/identity.json`, falling back to `~/.config/aperas/identity.json` when that variable is unset — the same place any other CLI tool's machine-local preferences go. Holds `machineNumber` (replacing the env-var-only `APERAS_MACHINE_NUMBER`), which must differ across machines by construction; a shared/committed home for it would make every clone inherit the same number and silently collide on generated ids.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQE8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDHSJQEG001",
    "text": "`aperas.config.json` — shared, committed, sits at a graph's own root: the directory directly containing that graph's `artifacts/` and `Apeiron/` (for this repo, `AperasKG/aperas.config.json`, `{ \"graph\": \".\" }`). Describes that graph's place in a tree (corp > teams > members, each mergeable into its parent). Its `graph` field takes either shape: a plain path (shorthand — both `artifacts/` and `Apeiron/` co-located directly under it, as above) or `{ \"apeiron\": \"<path>\", \"artifacts\": \"<path>\" }` (two independently-specifiable roots, for a graph whose data and markdown tree aren't co-located). `parent`: a path/url to the graph above, or absent for a root graph. Discovered the way `git` finds `.git` — walking up from a starting directory, nearest file wins — so a member's own graph is found before its team's, a team's before its corp's.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQE8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG002:links:Link:00CDHSJR88000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDHSCK8R001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQEG002:links:Link:00CDHSJR88000:props:StringProp:00CDXSREF0000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "484"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDHSJQEG002",
    "text": "Both replace `getArtifactsDir()`'s original `__dirname`-relative hop-counting (correct only for \"the CLI's own source sits a fixed number of directories below the one graph it drives\" — never true for an installed CLI, and not expressive enough for a tree of graphs regardless); a config-free checkout (no `aperas.config.json` found anywhere above the starting directory) falls back to that same hop-counted path unmigrated, so this repo's own dev setup keeps working either way. See [discussion](../discussion/packaging.md#id/BlockNode:00CDHSCK8R001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQE8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
