        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDWNYSRG002",
    "text": "`identity.json` (`@aperas/core/identity`) wired into `snowflake.ts`'s machine-number lookup (env var still wins if set, then identity.json, then the same defaulting warning as before) — plus `aperas identity show`/`set <n>` (`kgIdentity.ts`), since a reader/writer with no way to actually invoke the writer wasn't really usable. Verified on this machine: the \"APERAS_MACHINE_NUMBER not set\" warning that printed on every single command all session is gone after `aperas identity set 0`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDWNYSRR000",
    "text": "Every `kgX.ts`'s `printHelp`/missing-arg usage strings rewritten from `kg:X --` to `aperas <verb>` (both the `--help` path and the plain missing-required-arg fallback, since leaving one fixed and the other stale would be a half-done job). `npm run verify`'s full 18-check suite still passes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "`packages/cli/build.mjs`",
    "text": "`packages/cli/build.mjs`: `esbuild` bundles `aperas.ts` (+ `core` transitively) to `packages/cli/dist/aperas.js`, `oxigraph` external, shebang banner, plus a generated minimal `dist/package.json` (name `aperas`, `bin`, `oxigraph` as a real dependency). Verified the bundle actually runs standalone — `node packages/cli/dist/aperas.js tree`/`backlinks`/`flush`, no `tsx` anywhere — against the real graph.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDWNYSRR002",
    "text": "**What the build verification actually exposed, and had to be fixed for it to work at all** (not scope creep — the build was correctly planned to be verified standalone, and it didn't work on the first real attempt):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRG001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR003",
      "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR004",
      "BlockNode:00CDWNYSS0000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR003:props:StringProp:00CE598QKR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR003:props:StringProp:00CE598QM0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CDWNYSRR003",
    "text": "`spawnService()` hardcoded spawning `tsx` + a separate `service.ts` file — which doesn't exist once bundled (`service.ts`'s code is inlined into the one `aperas.js`, no file of its own anymore). Fixed: `service.ts` now exports its own `main`; `aperas.ts` recognizes a hidden `--__service` verb (checked before normal dispatch) that dynamically imports and runs it — the dynamic import is also what makes `esbuild` include `service.ts`'s code in the bundle at all, since nothing else reachable from `aperas.ts` ever imported it; `spawnService()` now detects dev-vs-built (does `service.ts` still exist as a sibling file?) and either spawns `tsx service.ts` (dev, unchanged) or re-invokes the running bundle itself with plain `node` + `--__service` (built).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDWNYSRR004",
    "text": "`serviceLock.ts`'s `getRunDir()`, `apeironNgn/store.ts`'s `getApeironExportDir()`, and `artifacts.ts`'s `getArtifactsDir()` all compute their path via a *hardcoded* number of `../` hops from their own file's location — correct only as long as that file sits exactly as deep as it did when the hop count was written. `esbuild` bundling collapses everything into one file at `packages/cli/dist/`, a different depth than the original source tree, so a fixed hop count silently pointed at the *wrong directory* once built rather than erroring — caught concretely: `getRunDir()`'s lock/socket landed one level *above* the actual workspace root entirely (a real, empty-looking `.run/` sitting right where it was expected masked this at first). Fixed all three with an `existsSync`-based dev-vs-built check (does a known dev-only sibling file still exist?), each branching to the right hop count for its actual layout.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSS0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDWNYSS0000",
    "text": "`computeCodeFingerprint()` (`codeVersion.ts`) unconditionally `readdirSync`'d `packages/{core,cli}/src` to build its staleness fingerprint — paths that don't exist at all once bundled, which would have crashed every single invocation of the built binary immediately. Fixed: returns a fixed `'built'` fingerprint when no dev source tree is found (nothing to detect as \"stale\" in a published build anyway — the code *is* whatever was installed).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDWNYSRR002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
