    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDWP69B8007",
    "text": "`serviceLock.ts`'s `getRunDir()`, `apeironNgn/store.ts`'s `getApeironExportDir()`, and `artifacts.ts`'s `getArtifactsDir()` all compute their path via a *hardcoded* number of `../` hops from their own file's location — correct only as long as that file sits exactly as deep as it did when the hop count was written. `esbuild` bundling collapses everything into one file at `packages/cli/dist/`, a different depth than the original source tree, so a fixed hop count silently pointed at the *wrong directory* once built rather than erroring — caught concretely: `getRunDir()`'s lock/socket landed one level *above* the actual workspace root entirely (a real, empty-looking `.run/` sitting right where it was expected masked this at first). Fixed all three with an `existsSync`-based dev-vs-built check (does a known dev-only sibling file still exist?), each branching to the right hop count for its actual layout.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDWP69B8005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWP69B8008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CDWP69B8008",
    "text": "`computeCodeFingerprint()` (`codeVersion.ts`) unconditionally `readdirSync`'d `packages/{core,cli}/src` to build its staleness fingerprint — paths that don't exist at all once bundled, which would have crashed every single invocation of the built binary immediately. Fixed: returns a fixed `'built'` fingerprint when no dev source tree is found (nothing to detect as \"stale\" in a published build anyway — the code *is* whatever was installed).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDWP69B8005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWPCC3G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Settled: no concurrency — one active graph at a time, explicit start/restart only",
    "text": "Clarified a confusion in the write-up above: a service holding a graph that's stable for its whole lifetime *was* the intent all along — the actual bug was that any command could *silently* auto-spawn one, so whichever command happened to run first invisibly decided the binding for as long as that process lived. The fix isn't to thread cwd through every request (that would mean re-resolving, i.e. potentially re-binding, on every call — not stable, and not the intent); it's to make the one moment of binding explicit and human-driven instead of accidental.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CDDTBQWG000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDX5H5MG002",
      "BlockNode:00CDX5H5MG003",
      "BlockNode:00CDX5H5MG004",
      "BlockNode:00CDX5H5MG005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWQ0V88001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWQ0V88001:props:StringProp:00CDWQ0W88000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWQ0V88001:props:StringProp:00CDWQ0W88001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T12:40:49.057Z",
    "title": "00CDWQ0V88001",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDDVC8N0007",
    "type": "list",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWQ0V88002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWQ0V88002:props:StringProp:00CE59A980000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
