      }
    ],
    "title": "**Inserting a list item always spawns a fresh run-leader**",
    "text": "**Inserting a list item always spawns a fresh run-leader**: <a name='id/BlockNode:00CE32GRZG002' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a> live-tested while writing this doc's own Resolved section — `aperas insert` piping a single new bullet always parses that content as its own orphaned one-item list, so the new item gets its own explicit `orderedList`/`startIndex` from the normalization rule (see Design). That marks it as a run-leader relative to whatever precedes it, even when the preceding item is an ordinary continuation of the very same list with the identical value — the render-time boundary rule (a next item's own explicit prop starts a new run) then treats it as a *second* run, rendering a spurious blank line before it. Not data corruption (bullet type and numbering both stay correct), but a real regression versus the old anchor-owns-it model, where a freshly inserted item carried no prop of its own and silently joined whatever run it landed in. No fix designed yet — needs its own decision (e.g. `aperas insert` clearing a freshly-parsed single item's own `orderedList`/`startIndex` when landing among existing plain `listItem` siblings, so it only carries the prop when it's genuinely starting something new). Confirmed live twice more since: pushing an existing list's *full* corrected content (rather than a bare `--after` insert) avoids it, since the new item then joins the same parsed run as a continuation instead of its own orphaned list — see [Freeflow](../discussion/list-consumption.md#id/BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR008) for the narrative.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GRZG000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
