    ],
    "title": "**The unified law**",
    "text": "**The unified law**: a list attaches to whatever most immediately precedes it, textually — a separate node if one exists (what looks like \"consumption\": the list nests *inside* that node), or the enclosing container's own merged identity if its leading paragraph was already consumed into it (what looks like \"dissolution\": the list's items become the container's *own* children, flat). These are the same rule, not two competing models — they only differ in whether \"the nearest preceding text\" turned out to be a standalone node or the container itself.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH0008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**When no consumer exists at all — dissolve anyway**",
    "text": "**When no consumer exists at all — dissolve anyway**: nothing precedes the list (a code block or blockquote sits directly above it, or it's literally the first content with no leading paragraph) — the list still dissolves into flat children of its true structural parent, at the position it occupied. There is no case in this model where a bare, unaddressable `list` wrapper is the *correct* outcome, only cases where today's code hasn't been extended to avoid one yet.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH0008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CE4757W0004",
    "text": "**Superseded: normalize to \"the run's own first item always owns `orderedList`/`startIndex`\", universally**: not just for orphan-dissolution as first proposed. Checked the actual code before deciding: `project.ts`'s `renderChildren` already has the run's first item in scope as its loop cursor at the exact point it reads these props — reading from it unconditionally removes a fallback branch, not adds one. `astParser.ts`'s three adoption branches (consumption, self-dissolution, orphan) currently need three different `setProp` targets — self-dissolution specifically threads a dedicated `parentListProps` field back through `ChildrenResult` solely because the container node doesn't exist yet when the list is seen. Normalizing collapses all three into one `setProp(items[0], ...)` call, run once before any branch, and removes `parentListProps` from the interface entirely. Also uncovered a real, intentional test in `verify.ts` (\"Expected the heading itself to carry the adopted list's orderedList/startIndex props\") asserting today's anchor-owns-it convention — needs rewriting regardless, since the orphan-dissolution work already breaks its adjacent-lists test. Cost: 48 existing anchors (18 headings, 18 listItems, 12 paragraphs) need their props relocated down to their own first child, beyond the 59 `list` nodes already in scope — same migration operation, just wider. Decided: normalize.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH0008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
