    "title": "**Started from two live mistakes**",
    "text": "**Started from two live mistakes**: while working `archive-migration`'s own [`treeview` sub-task](../discussion/archive-migration.md#id/BlockNode:00CE0HD0HG007), `aperas insert`/`update` targeting a `list` node directly twice produced an accidental nested-list-in-list, once for a Task Breakdown item and once for a History milestone. Both recovered by targeting the parent heading with full corrected content instead — but that workaround doesn't scale once a freeflow tree grows deep, which is what prompted asking whether the `list` container could be eliminated at the root. One of the two gaps logged there (\"`List` isn't consumed by a preceding `Heading`\") is this design's own direct origin — see [Issues](../issues/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW638008), now resolved by it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000G"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000G:props:StringProp:00CE598HW8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000G:props:StringProp:00CE598HW8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "Corpus survey before proposing anything",
    "text": "Corpus survey before proposing anything: 54 `list` nodes vs. 307 `listItem`s; 159 items (52%) already have no `list` parent — parented directly by a heading, another listItem, a paragraph, or an artifact, and all of it projects correctly. Nothing depends on a list node's identity (zero `Link`s target one, zero `TreeView.unfolds` entries name one). The only payload a container carries is `orderedList`/`startIndex`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**First proposal — overbuilt**",
    "text": "**First proposal — overbuilt**: drop `list` entirely, put `ordered`/`startIndex` on every item unconditionally, migrate via a splice-fixpoint. Motivated by a hypothesized \"one parent, two lists, one prop slot\" collision.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000J"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000J:props:StringProp:00CE598HGG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000J:props:StringProp:00CE598HGG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "Corrected on inspection of `astParser.ts`'s actual adoption logic (§8)",
    "text": "Corrected on inspection of `astParser.ts`'s actual adoption logic (§8): `adoptionAnchor` resets to `null` after *every* list, adopted or orphaned — specifically to stop \"two lists directly adjacent... incorrectly merg[ing] into a single adoption target, corrupting whichever `orderedList`/`startIndex` was set first\" (the code's own comment, verbatim). A second list under the same parent either adopts into a different, later anchor, or becomes its own separate orphaned node — never a bare run merged with the first. The hypothesized collision doesn't occur for consumption/self-dissolution; per-item props would have solved a problem that can't happen there.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000H",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The consumption/dissolution distinction, surfaced by a concrete counter-example**",
    "text": "**The consumption/dissolution distinction, surfaced by a concrete counter-example**: `# H1 / > Blockquote / paragraph text / - item1 / - item2` — traced through `convertChildren`: the paragraph sits at index 1 (blockquote is first), so it's a real emitted node, and the list adopts *into* it (`H1 { Blockquote, paragraph { item1, item2 } }`), not flattened up to be siblings of the paragraph. Confirmed the same shape live in this very conversation: a reply's own \"(a)/(b)\" list, immediately following a colon-terminated lead-in paragraph two paragraphs below an unrelated one, parses (by the same rule a human reader applies) as children of the *nearest* paragraph, not the more distant one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000M",
      "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000N"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000M:props:StringProp:00CE598H70000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000M:props:StringProp:00CE598H70001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "Checked whether this nesting is even visible in projected markdown",
    "text": "Checked whether this nesting is even visible in projected markdown: it isn't — `project.ts`'s `paragraph` case adds no indentation for adopted children, so consumption and dissolution render byte-identical. The difference is purely graph shape, invisible in the `.md` file — same as the container question itself.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000K",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000N:links:Link:00CE343EJG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG006",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000N:links:Link:00CE343EJG000:props:StringProp:00CE343EJG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "37"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "But graph shape isn't cosmetic here",
    "text": "But graph shape isn't cosmetic here: [design/treeview.md](../design/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG006)'s own fold rule cascades through a node's structural children. Whichever node genuinely owns a list's items is the node whose fold/unfold reaches them — so nesting depth has real behavioral consequences even though it's invisible on disk.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000K",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Settled**",
    "text": "**Settled**: consumption is correct as designed — it matches ordinary reading comprehension, not an accident to flatten away. The only remaining gap is the no-anchor-at-all case, and even that should dissolve rather than stay wrapped — see Settled, above.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A third live mistake, while writing this very entry**",
    "text": "**A third live mistake, while writing this very entry**: piping plain text with no leading `- ` marker into a list-target `insert` parsed as a bare `paragraph`, not a `listItem` — breaking the run in two instead of extending it (verified via the actual projected output: the new entry rendered with no bullet, no indentation, as a stray paragraph between two list runs). Recovered the same two-step way as a wrongly-created nested list: create, promote the real item out via `insert <item-id> --after <sibling>`, tombstone the wrapper.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
