  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE4757W0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CE4757W0005",
    "text": "**Run boundaries at render time are marked by an item's own explicit props, not by type alone**: verified live against the project's own parser — `# H` followed directly by a bullet list then an ordered list (no blank line, no separator) produces two adjacent mdast `list` nodes with nothing between them, exactly the case the original `adoptionAnchor`-reset comment names. Once both dissolve as orphans, their items land back-to-back in the same parent's children, all typed `listItem` — a boundary-scan keyed on type alone (`while (children[j].type === 'listItem') j++`) would merge them into one run and read only the first item's props, silently losing the second list's ordered-ness. Fix: a run ends not just where the type changes, but wherever the *next* item carries its own explicit `orderedList` (`getProp(item, 'orderedList') !== undefined`, distinguishing a real per-item value from an inherited/absent one) — every dissolved run's first item already gets this set at parse time regardless of adjacency, so the render-side scan only needs to consult it, not compute anything new. Specific to orphan-dissolution: consumption and self-dissolution can never produce two runs under one parent, so this case never arises for either.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH0008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE47F32G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "A run of adjacent `listItem`s doesn't always mean one list",
    "text": "A run of adjacent `listItem`s doesn't always mean one list: two lists can sit directly adjacent in the source with nothing between them (a bullet list immediately followed by an ordered list, no blank line — a real, common markdown shape, not a contrived edge case), and once both dissolve as orphans their items land back-to-back under the same parent, all typed `listItem` with no structural marker separating them. A run therefore ends not just where the type changes, but wherever the *next* item carries its own explicit `orderedList`/`startIndex` — a run-leader signal every dissolved run's first item already carries regardless of adjacency. This case is specific to orphan-dissolution: consumption and self-dissolution can never produce two runs under one parent, since only one thing can ever adopt into, or merge into, one given anchor.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GQM8008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5B6A58001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5B6A58001:links:Link:00CE5D1C78000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CE32GW3G002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5B6A58001:links:Link:00CE5D1C78000:props:StringProp:00CE5D1C78001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "684"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CE678B70003",
    "text": "**Orphaned lists dissolve, `orderedList`/`startIndex` normalized onto the run's own first item**: `astParser.ts`'s orphan branch now splices items flat into the true parent instead of wrapping a `list`-typed node, and all three adoption branches set props uniformly via one `setProp(items[0], ...)` call. `project.ts`'s `renderChildren` reads props from each run's own first item and ends a run at a type change or the next item's own explicit `orderedList` — verified against two synthetic cases (a separator between two runs, and two runs directly adjacent with none) plus the full real corpus (48 legacy anchors relocated, 59 live `list` nodes fixpoint-spliced and tombstoned; see [History](../history/list-consumption.md#id/BlockNode:00CE32GW3G002)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GRZG007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5BVP6R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
