      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5BVP6R001:props:StringProp:00CE5BVQAR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "Implemented and migrated",
    "text": "Implemented and migrated: `astParser.ts`'s `convertChildren` normalizes `orderedList`/`startIndex` onto the run's own first converted item uniformly across all three cases, and the orphan branch splices items flat instead of wrapping a `list`-typed node; `project.ts`'s `renderChildren` reads props from each run's own first item and ends a run at a type change or the next item's own explicit `orderedList` prop. A one-off migration script relocated props off the 48 legacy anchors and fixpoint-spliced all 59 live `list` nodes into their parents (tombstoned, kept per the never-splice-out-of-children convention); verified via a full before/after projection diff across all 28 tracked artifacts, byte-identical except one pre-existing, unrelated quirk in `issues/packaging.md` (see [Resolved](../issues/list-consumption.md#id/BlockNode:00CE32GRZG007)). `npm run verify` passes clean against the migrated corpus, including three new synthetic tests (consumption, zero-separator dissolution, separated-runs dissolution).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GW3G003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5D1A10004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**`aperas insert <list-id>` piping a bare `- item` line created a nested list-in-list**",
    "text": "**`aperas insert <list-id>` piping a bare `- item` line created a nested list-in-list**: confirmed moot post-migration — live-tested against the migrated corpus (inserting a bare bullet after an existing listItem) creates a plain sibling `listItem`, no wrapper. There is no longer a live `list`-typed node anywhere in the corpus to mistakenly target this way.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GRZG007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5D1A10005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**`aperas insert <list-id>` piping plain text with no leading `- ` marker created a wrongly-typed `paragraph`**",
    "text": "**`aperas insert <list-id>` piping plain text with no leading `- ` marker created a wrongly-typed `paragraph`**: this half was never actually about list-node existence — piping text with no bullet marker still parses as a `paragraph`, migration or not (live-tested). Not a list-consumption bug: it's the parser correctly doing what the input told it to do. A caution for the `aperas` skill's own insert guidance, not something this design can fix.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GRZG007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5D1A10006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**`verify.ts` hard-asserted the old anchor-owns-it convention**",
    "text": "**`verify.ts` hard-asserted the old anchor-owns-it convention**: rewritten — \"Testing consumption\", \"Testing dissolution when orphaned, zero-separator variant\", and \"Testing dissolution when orphaned, separated variant\" replace the old \"heading consume + list adoption\"/\"adjacent lists\" checks, asserting props on each run's own first item and dissolution into flat children.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GRZG007",
