    "title": "00CE5EHVXR001",
    "text": "**Migration debugging, three real bugs found and fixed along the way**: none of the three were design errors — each surfaced only once the migration script actually ran against the real corpus, not a synthetic sample.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR002",
      "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR003",
      "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR002:props:StringProp:00CE5EHX5R002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR002:props:StringProp:00CE5EHX5R003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "Ordering bug",
    "text": "Ordering bug: relocating the 48 legacy anchors' props (Part B) after fixpoint-splicing the 59 orphaned lists (Part A) let Part A write a *fresh* `orderedList` onto a listItem that also happened to be one of the 48 — Part B then misread that fresh value as stale legacy data and tried to relocate it a second time, onto a nonexistent child. Fixed by running Part B first, snapshotting all 48 values upfront before any writes (a listItem anchor's own first child can itself be another one of the 48 — a nested adoption — so reads and writes had to be fully decoupled, not just reordered).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Stale-tombstone-as-items[0] bug",
    "text": "Stale-tombstone-as-items[0] bug: a list nested inside another live list (a pre-existing wart from an earlier `aperas insert <list-id>` mistake) can, once *its own* nested list resolves in an earlier fixpoint pass, be left holding an already-tombstoned marker among its own `children`. Left unfiltered, that dead marker could land at `items[0]` and silently take the run-leader prop meant for a real item — found live via `issues/packaging.md`'s \"Resolved\" list, a genuine 3-deep chain of exactly this wart. Fixed by filtering tombstoned entries out of `items` before processing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Contaminated-baseline bug (methodology, not code)",
    "text": "Contaminated-baseline bug (methodology, not code): the migration script's own \"before\" snapshot was computed by re-projecting the *not-yet-migrated* graph through the *already-updated* `project.ts` — which reads props from each run's own first item, a place not-yet-migrated data doesn't have them yet. That's not a valid baseline; it's exactly the inconsistency the migration exists to fix. Corrected to read the real checked-in files off disk as \"before,\" `project.ts`'s new code only ever applied to the \"after\" snapshot.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The one accepted exception, traced to its root**",
    "text": "**The one accepted exception, traced to its root**: `issues/packaging.md`'s \"## Pending Tasks\" holds a 4-level-deep chain of `list` wrapper nodes that are entirely dead — every real listItem/paragraph inside was tombstoned individually over time as those tasks were resolved (and re-stated more clearly in \"## Resolved\"), but the empty wrapper shells were never cleaned up. The old renderer's `renderChildren` returns `''` for a genuinely empty list, but `.join('\\n\\n')`-ing two such `''`-rendering siblings manufactures a real blank line out of nothing, compounding through each level of nesting into 5 spurious blank lines before `## Resolved`. Confirmed via full-chain inspection (`BlockNode.jsonld` reads at every level) and reproduced by hand-tracing `renderChildren`'s recursion. The migration correctly tombstones the whole dead chain with nothing live left in it, so it renders as truly empty — a strict improvement, no visible content changed, approved by the user before letting the migration's diff-gate through for this one file.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T13:03:56.715Z",
    "title": "**Tangential finding: a fully-dead container is never automatically cleaned up**",
    "text": "**Tangential finding: a fully-dead container is never automatically cleaned up**: asked directly why a service restart hadn't already collected `issues/packaging.md`'s dead wrapper chain — it's not a GC/reachability bug. `pruneUnreachableTombstones` only collects nodes that are *themselves* tombstoned and unreachable; here, individual completed items were tombstoned one at a time as they were resolved, but nothing ever tombstones the *container* once its last live child is gone. The now-vacuous wrapper just sits there, live, referencing nothing meaningful, invisible to any restart or GC pass since it was never itself marked dead. A real, if minor, process gap (completing/removing the last item in a list should probably also collapse the now-empty container) — belongs in a general crud/tombstone-lifecycle concern once one exists in the graph (`crud.md` is still archived, next on `archive-migration`'s own list), not specific to list-consumption.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T13:03:57.507Z",
    "title": "00CE5EHVXR007",
    "text": "**Tangential finding: `aperas update`'s reconciliation can silently match fresh content onto an already-tombstoned node's id without reviving it**: while writing this doc set's own Resolved section, a new bullet's content landed on the id of an unrelated, already-tombstoned test-probe node from earlier live-testing — the reconciler apparently reused that dead slot (likely a positional fallback match, not a content match) but never cleared `tombstonedAt`, so the new content silently rendered as invisible until caught by noticing the Resolved section had one fewer visible item than pushed. Recovered by directly clearing `tombstonedAt` on the specific node once identified. Not investigated further (the root cause in `reconcile.ts`'s matching logic is unclear — content-key mismatch makes a content-based match implausible, so it's likely a positional/leftover-slot fallback that doesn't check for tombstoned status before reusing an id) — worth its own investigation whenever CRUD/reconciliation gets its own dedicated pass.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5EHVXR008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
