  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE5KSNVR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CE5KSNVR003",
    "text": "**`aperas update`'s reconciliation can silently match fresh content onto an already-tombstoned node's id without reviving it**: while writing this doc set's own Resolved section, a new bullet's content landed on the id of an unrelated, already-tombstoned test-probe node from earlier live-testing — the reconciler apparently reused that dead slot (likely a positional fallback match, not a content match) but never cleared `tombstonedAt`, so the new content silently rendered as invisible until caught by noticing the Resolved section had one fewer visible item than pushed. Recovered by directly clearing `tombstonedAt` on the specific node once identified. Reproduced a second time, identical shape: pushing the same Resolved heading's full content plus one new item, right after tombstoning an unrelated single-item probe added moments earlier, landed the new item on that exact same tombstoned id again — strongly suggesting the fallback prefers reusing *any* available tombstoned child in the heading's own children array over minting a fresh id for a genuinely new, unmatched entry, regardless of content. Parked here with no real home yet — the root cause in `reconcile.ts`'s matching logic needs its own investigation whenever CRUD/reconciliation gets a dedicated pass.",
