          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**History**",
    "text": "**History**: [Current Status](../history/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEA5MHD8002) — what exists today.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5J8002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Architecture",
    "text": "The skill teaches one thing, and everything else in it is a consequence: **Apeiron is the source, and the `.md` files under `artifacts/` are shadows projected from it.** A projection can be regenerated at any time; it is never where a change is made.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5J8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG004",
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG009"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Four levels, each following from the one above",
    "text": "Four levels, each following from the one above:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG005",
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG006",
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG007",
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG008"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG005:props:StringProp:00CEA5Y6QR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG005:props:StringProp:00CEA5Y6QR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEA5Y5JG005",
    "text": "**Philosophy** — the graph is the source, and its substance is nodes together with the links between them. A node's meaning is constituted by what it links to, what links back, and where it sits, not by its own text alone.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEA5Y5JG006",
    "text": "**Orientation** — traversal is therefore the primary mode of work rather than a check appended to it. Reading means following links; writing means placing them. `archive/Aperas-design.md`'s Multi-Agent Projection Pattern names both as the architecture's own capabilities. *Deep read* runs in two directions answering different questions: forward and downward — a block's children and its outgoing links — is deeper **content**, what the block is made of and refers to, and is what an ordinary read wants; backward along backlinks is deeper **context**, who depends on this and what surrounds it, reached for when the decision is harder than reading. The tool surface already carves it this way: `aperas unfold` previews children and forward links together because both are content, while `backlinks` stands alone because it is the other axis. *Deep write* is inherently backward — what a change breaks is only answerable from the citing side. *Dense linking* is the precondition for all of it: a sparsely linked graph gives deep read nothing to descend into and deep write nothing to follow. These are crystallized practice rather than theory, but the practice predates this system — years of real knowledge-graph work in Logseq, carried in as design instead of rediscovered here. That this system has barely exercised them yet is a fact about its infancy, not their standing: the same bottom-up emergence that turns an incident into a skill item, running at the scale of a whole architecture.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG007:links:Link:00CF110A9G000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDC0D59000B",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG007:links:Link:00CF110A9G000:props:StringProp:00CF110A9R000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "295"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Discipline** — how to behave once oriented",
    "text": "**Discipline** — how to behave once oriented: edit graph-first, target the smallest block that actually changed, write discussion before executing, stage each verified step, respect citation direction, prefer a move over remove-and-recreate wherever identity should survive — the same guarantee [linking's rename mechanism](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDC0D59000B) makes structurally, by adding an anchor rather than replacing one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG008:links:Link:00CF10XA90000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDBYV4T8002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG008:links:Link:00CF10XA90000:props:StringProp:00CF10XA90001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "75"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Mechanics** — how to type it",
    "text": "**Mechanics** — how to type it: CLI verbs and their path-resolution rules, [wikilink and anchor syntax](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T8002), `--after`/`--before` parentage, shell quoting, and the current tool gaps with their workarounds.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEA5Y5JG009",
    "text": "An item belongs at the level that explains *why* it is true, not the level where it was first noticed. A shell-quoting bug is Mechanics. Treating a projected file as the source is Philosophy — and its recurrence is evidence that the first level has not landed, which no quantity of Mechanics will repair.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Topology",
    "text": "The four levels are the document's own top-level structure, in that order, abstract to concrete. The ordering is load-bearing rather than cosmetic: a reader who stops after the first two levels should already act correctly in the ordinary case, while a reader who starts at the bottom acquires a list of gotchas with nothing to hang them on.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5J8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00B",
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00C",
      "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0001",
      "BlockNode:00CEDF5XVR001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEA5Y5JG00B",
    "text": "Within a level, each item is stated rule-first, with its originating incident attached rather than leading. The incident must travel with the rule, since a rule whose cause is lost gets misapplied at its edges or cargo-culted past its expiry — but it should not have to be excavated before the rule can be read.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00A",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Every item carries its status where it stands",
    "text": "Every item carries its status where it stands: confirmed-current, superseded by a change that has since landed, or identified-but-unverified. A caution whose cause has been removed is worse than no caution, because it still costs the reader something and no longer buys anything.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00A",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Workflows",
    "text": "Two scopes run through this section, and they coincide rather than merely resembling one another. The **object workflow** is what the skill teaches an agent to do while working the graph. The **meta workflow** is how the skill document itself is grown and maintained. Growth below is purely meta; the edit loop is purely object; citation direction and crystallization are object rules that the skill's own upkeep then obeys in turn. `archive/Aperas-design.md` calls this coincidence Meta-Aperas — the platform builds itself using the exact principles it uses to manage knowledge — so the overlap is the design working as intended, not a section wanting to be split in two.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5J8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEB8N5EG001",
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00E",
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00G",
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00J"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Growth — bottom-up emergence",
    "text": "The skill accretes from live incidents, never from reasoning in the abstract. An item travels a fixed path: an incident occurs during real work; it is captured raw in the task's own freeflow discussion, before it is understood rather than after; it is then confirmed, by reproduction or by tracing it to a cause in the code; and only then is it promoted into the skill, at the level that explains it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00D",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00F",
      "BlockNode:00CEDF24W0001",
      "BlockNode:00CEDF24W0002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEA5Y5JG00F",
    "text": "The first two steps are non-negotiable in that order. A finding written up once the work has concluded has already lost the specifics that made it worth keeping.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00G:links:Link:00CF10T8HG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CDD68E8000K",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00G:links:Link:00CF10T8HG000:props:StringProp:00CF10T8HG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "414"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "### Citation direction",
    "text": "The concerns form an abstraction gradient, `design` most abstract and `discussion` least, with `issues`, `planning` and `history` between. A citation may point up that gradient or sideways along it freely. It may not point down: a design block does not reference a discussion block, for the same reason a node carries a parent pointer and not a list of children. The concern taxonomy's own `# Context` convention ([documentation](../design/documentation.md#id/BlockNode:00CDD68E8000K): \"one link plus a brief, non-argumentative summary per doc\") already states this same up-only citation shape for that one sanctioned exception, independently of this rule.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00D",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00H"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEA5Y5JG00H",
    "text": "The one sanctioned exception is a designated index. A design doc's `# Context` section exists precisely to index its own concern's other facets, one link each — a single structurally-privileged reference, not a citation scattered through the body.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00G",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Crystallization — content and topology",
    "text": "Crystallization matures a thought along two axes at once.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00D",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00K",
      "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00M"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEA5Y5JG00K",
    "text": "On the **content** axis, prose moves from freeform discussion into normative design, issues, planning, or history.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00J",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEA5Y5JG00M",
    "text": "On the **topology** axis, a relationship that first existed *indirectly* — mediated by a discussion node that cites both ends and carries the judgment in its own text — becomes a *direct* citation between those ends once it proves load-bearing rather than incidental, and the mediating node's work is then done. Link density is the product of this lifecycle, which is why it reads as maturity from outside rather than as tidiness.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00J",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG002:links:Link:00CEA68S28002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG002:links:Link:00CEA68S28002:props:StringProp:00CEA68S28003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "0"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG002:props:StringProp:00CEA68S28000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG002:props:StringProp:00CEA68S28001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEA68QVG002",
    "text": "[Design](../design/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG003) — the four-level shape, abstract to concrete, the rewrite is aiming at.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG003:links:Link:00CEA68S2G000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG003:links:Link:00CEA68S2G000:props:StringProp:00CEA68S2G001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "0"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEA68QVG003",
    "text": "[Issues](../issues/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G002) — where today's flat v0 falls short of it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG004:links:Link:00CEA68S2G002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S8005",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG004:links:Link:00CEA68S2G002:props:StringProp:00CEA68S2G003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "0"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEA68QVG004",
    "text": "[Planning](../planning/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEA5M6S8005) — the ordered work to get there.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG005:links:Link:00CEA68S2G004",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CEA5MHD8002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CEA68QVG005:links:Link:00CEA68S2G004:props:StringProp:00CEA68S2G005",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "0"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEA68QVG005",
    "text": "[History](../history/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEA5MHD8002) — what exists today.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB07XC0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Open Questions",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZJR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEB07XC0002",
      "BlockNode:00CE9SP5H0001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB07XC0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T19:20:47.347Z",
    "title": "00CEB07XC0002",
    "text": "placeholder",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB07XC0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Examples — the vertical thread",
    "text": "Examples are not a fifth level. They run vertically through all four, because a level stated only as theory is the failure mode the levels exist to prevent: a reader who has understood a principle abstractly but never seen it instantiated will still reach for the wrong tool under pressure.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00A",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0002",
      "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW8002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEB0XSW0002",
    "text": "Each level takes a different kind of example, and the kind is dictated by what that level has to teach.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0003",
      "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0004",
      "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW8000",
      "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW8001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0003:props:StringProp:00CEB0XV2R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0003:props:StringProp:00CEB0XV2R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEB0XSW0003",
    "text": "**Philosophy** takes a *contrast pair* — the same task done both ways, wrong then right, so the principle registers as a lived distinction rather than a slogan. Grepping projected files against traversing the graph, with both transcripts shown and the cost of the first made visible, teaches the source/shadow distinction in a way no restatement of it can.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEB0XSW0004",
    "text": "**Orientation** takes a *short traversal transcript* — a real `aperas backlinks` answer, one hop followed, the thing found at the other end. This is what \"reading means following links\" looks like when it is happening, and it is short enough to read as a whole.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Discipline** takes *one full worked loop*, end to end",
    "text": "**Discipline** takes *one full worked loop*, end to end: locate the smallest block that changed, update it, verify via backlinks rather than the summary line, project, stage. Nearly every real task is this loop, so one complete instance carries further than five fragments.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEB0XSW8001",
    "text": "**Mechanics** takes the *minimal command plus its exact failure text*. The error string is half the example and often the more useful half — recognising `anchor is not a child of X` is what converts a five-minute detour into a five-second one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEB0XSW8002",
    "text": "Examples are harvested, never synthesised. The bottom-up growth path already produces them: every incident that earns an item is, by construction, a real transcript with a real error message attached. A synthesised example can be subtly wrong in ways nobody notices until it is followed — this corpus has already paid that price once, when a migration's \"before\" snapshot was computed through the already-updated serialiser and produced a baseline that looked plausible and was meaningless.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB0XSW0001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB17CPG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDFKN30007",
    "text": "Stage the repeatedly hand-written helpers as bundled `scripts/`, starting with a raw-node reader over `BlockNode.jsonld`/`ArtifactNode.jsonld` for the fields no CLI verb exposes. The skill currently instructs its own reinvention, which is the clearest possible signal the helper belongs staged rather than described. Replace that instruction with a pointer to the script, and keep the tracked toolset gap it is standing in for.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S8005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5EG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### The edit loop — object workflow",
    "text": "The shape of nearly every real task against the graph, and the thing a Discipline-level worked example should instantiate end to end.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00D",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER000",
      "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER001",
      "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER002",
      "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER003",
      "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER004",
      "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER000:props:StringProp:00CEB8N6BG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER000:props:StringProp:00CEB8N6BG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEB8N5ER000",
    "text": "**Orient before touching anything — content first, context when the decision is hard.** Start with `aperas unfold <ref>`, which previews the block's children *and* its forward links together: both are deeper **content**, the substance the block is made of and refers to, and that is what an ordinary read needs. Escalate to `aperas backlinks <id> --text` for deeper **context** — who depends on this block, who cites it, what surrounds it — when the decision is harder than reading: editing, or an investigation whose scope has widened. Editing is always such a case, since step 5 cannot find what a change breaks without it. A block's meaning is not contained in its own text, so an edit decided without both directions is decided on partial information.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5EG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEB8N5ER001",
    "text": "**Locate the smallest block that actually changed.** Not the artifact, not the enclosing heading: the leaf whose content is wrong. Anything larger means reconstructing unchanged siblings by hand, where a single transcription slip silently tombstones a block and mints a new id in its place.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5EG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEB8N5ER002",
    "text": "**Edit graph-first.** Pipe replacement content to `aperas update <id>`, or `aperas insert` for genuinely new content, and reposition with a stdin-less `insert` when a node should move rather than be recreated. The projected file is never the place an edit is made.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5EG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEB8N5ER003",
    "text": "**Verify by traversal, not by summary line.** A reconcile count reports what the command believes it did. A backlink that actually resolves, or a `--dry-run` projection where the content is actually visible, is what proves it happened.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5EG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEB8N5ER004",
    "text": "**Deep write — follow what the change made stale.** Check the block's backlinks and forward links and update what now disagrees with it. This is the step most easily skipped and the one that keeps the graph coherent rather than merely correct in one place.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5EG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5ER005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEB8N5ER005",
    "text": "**Project, then stage.** `aperas project <path> --flush`, then `git add` in `AperasKG/` straight away, so the index stands as a checkpoint if a later step goes wrong. Staging only — committing stays the user's call.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEB8N5EG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEC1JMWR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEC1JMWR001:props:StringProp:00CEC1JP48000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEC1JMWR001:props:StringProp:00CEC1JP48001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEC1JMWR001",
    "text": "**What having no convention actually costs — three concrete losses, not an aesthetic one**: *queryability*, since `aperas backlinks` returns citers undifferentiated and mixes content that merely references a node with judgments about it, which a reader wants separated; *lifecycle*, since crystallization ends a mediating node's job the moment its relationship becomes a direct citation, and nothing can find promotion candidates while indirect edges stay unidentifiable; and *deep read's context axis*, since judgments about a node are its highest-value context and currently surface buried among incidental mentions.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE9SP5H0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEC1JMWR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEC1JMWR002",
    "text": "**The option worth arguing for is narrower than the question's framing — a coarse predicate as classifier, with the claim staying in prose**: the plumbing already carries a predicate field sitting unused at the generic `[[wikilink]]`. Tagging such a link `judges` while the actual reasoning stays in the source node's own text restores machine-recognizability without re-importing the thing the `kg:assert` recall was right to remove.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE9SP5H0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEC1JMWR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**This would not undo that recall**",
    "text": "**This would not undo that recall**: `archive/Aperas-design.md`'s Graph view already specifies typed predicates (`impacts`, `verifies`, `derived_from`) as part of the design. Typed predicates were always intended; what was recalled was assertions-*as*-bare-triples — the claim being nothing but a triple — not predicates existing at all.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE9SP5H0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEC1JMWR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The counter-argument is Meta-Aperas itself**",
    "text": "**The counter-argument is Meta-Aperas itself**: crystallize an interface only when a real human–agent coordination point demands it, never ahead of one. This concern has produced two indirect-edge nodes so far. Two is not pressure.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE9SP5H0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEC1JMWR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Proposed resolution — settle the design, defer the implementation**",
    "text": "**Proposed resolution — settle the design, defer the implementation**: adopt the coarse-predicate-classifier shape as the intended answer so the decision is not re-litigated, and hold the implementation until there are enough indirect edges to genuinely want querying over them. That is a falsifiable trigger rather than a vague \"later\".",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE9SP5H0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG001:props:StringProp:00CECCBMX8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG001:props:StringProp:00CECCBMX8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CECCBKNG001",
    "text": "**`extractAnchorNames` treats a quoted example anchor as a real one — it has no idea what a code span is**: `astParser.ts`'s `extractAnchorNames(text)` is a bare regex (`ANCHOR_NAME_RE`) run over a block's raw text, with no AST and no markdown awareness, so an anchor tag appearing *inside backticks as an illustration* is indistinguishable from one the projector actually spliced in. `parsedBlockNames`/`liveBlockNames` then register that illustration as a name the block genuinely answers to, and `rejectSlugPathCollisions` enforces it. Found live: dumping the skill's own item 2 — which quotes a complete anchor tag (an `a` element whose `name` is an `id/BlockNode:` address and whose class is `aperas-anchor aperas-id`) precisely to warn readers off pasting projected files back in — made that block claim the quoted address as one of its own names. It still does: `BlockNode:00CE95MVP8008` holds it today. Only one claimant exists, so nothing collides yet, but any second document quoting the same example is rejected — and `design/linking.md`, the canonical spec for anchor syntax, is exactly the kind of document that must quote it. This is the source/shadow confusion reproduced inside the parser: text *depicting* an anchor taken for the anchor itself. Not a judgement call — a code span is literal display text by definition, so an anchor inside one is never a real claim; the regex needs to skip backtick-delimited spans.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG002",
      "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG002:props:StringProp:00CECCBMX8002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG002:props:StringProp:00CECCBMX8003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "Demonstrated by this very entry",
    "text": "Demonstrated by this very entry: it cannot quote the offending tag verbatim to describe it, because doing so would claim the same already-held name and be rejected on write. A bug severe enough that its own report has to work around it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CECCBKNG003",
    "text": "Same blind spot runs the other way through the shared regex: `stripInlineAnchors` is built from `ANCHOR_NAME_RE.source`, so it will also strip a *quoted* example out of any text it normalizes. It is applied in `extractAbstract` (artifact and folder abstracts) and in `reconcile.ts`'s `leafKey` (block-level Gestalt matching) — and an artifact's abstract is what scoped rename detection compares by exact equality. Flagged rather than asserted: the mechanism is the same defect, but the consequences on those two paths have not been checked live.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CECCBKNG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECFF700002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T21:04:00.885Z",
    "title": "00CECFF700002",
    "text": "**The document is flat where the subject matter is layered** — four levels now, Philosophy through Mechanics.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECFF700003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T21:04:01.868Z",
    "title": "00CECFF700003",
    "text": "**The load-bearing statement is last** — the graph-substance principle is promoted to Orientation, ahead of every mechanical rule that follows from it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECFF700004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T21:04:02.775Z",
    "title": "00CECFF700004",
    "text": "**Settled facts and live cautions are not distinguished** — every item is marked `[current]`, `[superseded]` or `[unverified]` where it stands.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECFF700005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T21:04:03.690Z",
    "title": "00CECFF700005",
    "text": "**The two section headings don't carve at a real joint** — \"Editing discipline\"/\"Working practices\" are gone, replaced by the level split.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECFF700006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T21:04:04.643Z",
    "title": "00CECFF700006",
    "text": "**Rule and origin are inconsistently ordered within items** — items are stated rule-first, incident attached.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECFF700007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T21:04:05.533Z",
    "title": "**No stated scope boundary** — stated in the skill",
    "text": "**No stated scope boundary** — stated in the skill: this skill owns working the graph, `kg-doc-ingest` owns getting existing text into it, project facts live in concern docs the skill points at.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CECFT3M8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-11T21:04:32.870Z",
    "title": "00CECFT3M8001",
    "text": "placeholder",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDEX35R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDEX35R001:props:StringProp:00CEDEX4A8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDEX35R001:props:StringProp:00CEDEX4A8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`skill-creator` complements this concern rather than competing with it**",
    "text": "**`skill-creator` complements this concern rather than competing with it**: it is a *process* skill — draft, test prompts, eval runs against a baseline, graded assertions, human review, description-trigger optimization — while this concern is an *architecture*: what shape the content takes, what belongs at which level, how an item earns entry. One supplies the instrument, the other the design, and neither substitutes for the other. Consistent with the scope boundary already settled: `skill-creator` is tooling-level, this concern is project-level.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA56A0G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDEX35R002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDEX35R002:links:Link:00CEDEX4AG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638006",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDEX35R002:links:Link:00CEDEX4AG000:props:StringProp:00CEDEX4AG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1217"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEDEX35R002",
    "text": "**Tooling emerges bottom-up too, and a bundled skill script is its staging area**: Growth already describes how *content* enters the skill — incident, captured raw in freeflow, confirmed, promoted to the level that explains it. Tooling has its own parallel path: **practice → skill script → discussion → issues → toolset update**. An ad-hoc one-liner rewritten in session after session is the practice; bundling it under the skill's own `scripts/` is the staging area — exactly as git staging sits between a working tree and a commit, reusable and shared but not yet part of the product; articulating why it keeps being needed is the discussion; tracking it as a real gap is the issue; absorbing it as a first-class CLI verb is the toolset update. The signal that something belongs on this path is repetition — the same helper written independently, session after session, which is a measurement rather than a matter of taste. Live instance: a Python reader over `BlockNode.jsonld` for `props`/`tombstonedAt`/children/exact-text was hand-written roughly eight times in a single session, while the skill itself instructed the reinvention (\"read it with a small one-liner\"); it belongs at the staging step, en route to [the tracked `aperas show <ref>` gap](../issues/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW638006).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA56A0G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDEX35R003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Splitting is decided by loading frequency, not by size**",
    "text": "**Splitting is decided by loading frequency, not by size**: progressive disclosure loads a skill in three stages — name and description always in context, the SKILL.md body once the skill triggers, bundled `references/` files only when actually needed. That maps straight onto the levels. Philosophy and Orientation belong in the body because their whole job is to land before anything else is read; Mechanics is lookup material, consulted only once a specific problem is hit, which makes it the natural `references/` candidate. This sharpens the earlier navigability criterion rather than replacing it: navigability is the symptom, loading frequency is the cause, and size is only a weak proxy for either.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA56A0G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDEX35R004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDEX35R004",
    "text": "**The Verification Plan's missing instrument is an eval loop with a real baseline**: the plan asserts something testable — an agent reading only the first two levels behaves correctly — but supplies no way to test it. An eval loop is that instrument: test prompts run both with the skill and against a baseline, graded on objective assertions, aggregated into a benchmark. Two things make this concern's case unusually clean. v0 is preserved in git history, so the baseline is the real previous version rather than no-skill-at-all, which measures the restructure itself rather than the skill's mere existence. And the prompts need not be invented, since real recorded incidents exist — the cross-reference question that produced the grep-instead-of-traverse failure, adding one item to an existing list, retitling a heading. Synthesizing a *test* is not the same as synthesizing an *example*: the harvest-don't-synthesize rule governs what goes into the document, not what probes it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA56A0G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDF24W0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Tooling emerges the same way, along its own path",
    "text": "Tooling emerges the same way, along its own path: **practice → skill script → discussion → issues → toolset update.** A helper written ad hoc during real work is the practice. Bundling it under the skill's own `scripts/` is a staging area — reusable and shared, but not yet part of the product, in the same sense that git staging sits between a working tree and a commit. Articulating why it keeps being needed is the discussion; tracking it as a real gap is the issue; absorbing it as a first-class CLI verb is the toolset update.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDF24W0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDF24W0002",
    "text": "The signal for entry onto this path is repetition, which is a measurement rather than a judgement: the same helper written independently, session after session. A skill that *instructs* the reinvention — telling the reader to write a one-liner it could simply have bundled — has identified the need and then declined to stage it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDF5XVR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### What lives in the body, and what gets deferred",
    "text": "A skill loads in three stages: name and description are always in context, the SKILL.md body arrives once the skill triggers, and bundled `references/` files are read only when actually needed. The levels map onto that directly, so **what to defer is decided by loading frequency, not by size.**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00A",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEDF5XVR002",
      "BlockNode:00CEDF5XW0000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDF5XVR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDF5XVR002",
    "text": "Philosophy and Orientation belong in the body unconditionally. Their whole job is to land before anything else is read, and a reader who never reaches them is the failure mode the level ordering exists to prevent. Discipline belongs there too, being the shape of ordinary work. Mechanics is different in kind: it is lookup material, consulted when a specific problem is hit rather than read through, which makes it the natural candidate for deferral into `references/` once its bulk starts crowding out the levels above it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEDF5XVR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDF5XW0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDF5XW0000",
    "text": "Size is only a weak proxy for this, and navigability is the symptom rather than the cause. The real question about any passage is whether a reader needs it *every* time or *occasionally* — and a level whose items are all consulted occasionally can be deferred wholesale without the reader losing anything they were relying on.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEDF5XVR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDFCMY0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDFCMY0001",
    "text": "The instrument for both checks is an eval loop, since neither is answerable from reading the document. Test prompts are run twice over — once by an agent holding the skill, once by a baseline agent — then graded against assertions and aggregated into a benchmark.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S800D",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDFCMY0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDFCMY0002",
    "text": "The baseline is the **previous version of the skill**, not the absence of one. v0 is preserved in git history, so the comparison measures the restructure itself rather than merely re-confirming that having a skill beats having none.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S800D",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDFCMY0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDFCMY0003",
    "text": "Prompts are harvested, not invented — real incidents already recorded in this concern's discussion supply them: the cross-reference question that produced the grep-instead-of-traverse failure, adding one item to an existing list, retitling a heading, linking two docs that resolve each other's gaps. Synthesizing a *test* is not the same as synthesizing an *example*; the harvest-don't-synthesize rule governs what goes into the document, not what probes it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S800D",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDFCMY0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Assertions must be objectively checkable, which these are",
    "text": "Assertions must be objectively checkable, which these are: did the run reach for `aperas backlinks` rather than `grep`; did a heading-text edit pass `--text-only`; did a list addition push the complete list; did the ids survive a move. Anything needing human judgement stays qualitative rather than being forced into an assertion.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S800D",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDFCMY8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDFCMY8000",
    "text": "Trigger accuracy is measured separately and last, once the content is settled. The frontmatter description is the only part of a skill always in context, so it alone decides whether the skill is consulted at all — a property that is empirically testable against labelled should-trigger and should-not-trigger queries, with a held-out split so the wording is not merely fitted to them, rather than settled by taste.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S800D",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDFKN30008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Defer by loading frequency once the body starts crowding",
    "text": "Defer by loading frequency once the body starts crowding: Philosophy, Orientation and Discipline stay in SKILL.md, Mechanics moves to `references/` when its bulk begins to push the levels above it out of easy reach. Not yet warranted — the body is well inside the point where deferral buys anything.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S8005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDFKN30009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDFKN30009",
    "text": "Build the eval set from recorded incidents, run it against the v0 baseline preserved in git history, grade on objective assertions, and review the benchmark. This is what actually closes the Verification Plan; until it runs, the claim that the levels change behaviour is untested.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S8005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDFKN3000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDFKN3000A",
    "text": "Measure the frontmatter description last, once the content has settled — labelled should-trigger and should-not-trigger queries with a held-out split, since triggering decides whether any of the rest is ever read.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5M6S8005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDP10P8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDP10P8001:props:StringProp:00CEDP120G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDP10P8001:props:StringProp:00CEDP120G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "The first tooling staging step was taken",
    "text": "The first tooling staging step was taken: `scripts/show_node.py` bundles the raw-node reader that had been hand-rewritten roughly eight times in a single session, covering the five uses that actually recurred — full record with `props`/`tombstonedAt`, exact stored text for round-tripping an edit, children with tombstones marked, full-text search standing in for the absent `aperas search`, and owning-artifact lookup. The skill's instruction to write \"a small one-liner\" is gone, replaced by a pointer. It is explicitly a staging area rather than a fix: the tracked gaps it stands in for (`aperas show <ref>`, and `unfold`'s missing tombstone marker) remain open.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5MHD8004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDT3AFG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDT3AFG000:props:StringProp:00CEDT3BRG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDT3AFG000:props:StringProp:00CEDT3BRR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEDT3AFG000",
    "text": "**One service per login session, but a per-process graph root — so graph isolation can only be serial**: `serviceLock.ts`'s `getRunDir()` puts the lock and socket at one fixed location (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/aperas`), deliberately independent of both `process.cwd()` and where the code is installed, with its own doc comment explaining why. Meanwhile `graphConfig.ts` lets `APERAS_APEIRON_ROOT`/`APERAS_ARTIFACTS_ROOT` override the roots per process. The two don't compose: once a service is running, a later call that sets those variables still reaches the service already bound to whatever graph started it, so the override is silently ineffective rather than rejected. Consequence: two agents cannot work different graphs concurrently, and anything that mutates a throwaway copy has to stop and rebind the shared service first — verified live, including that a write then lands in the clone and not in the live corpus. Worked around for now by `skills/aperas-workspace/graph-sandbox.sh` (reset clone to a tag, rebind, run one round, rebind back), which makes the isolation explicit and reversible but keeps it strictly serial. A real fix would derive the socket path from the resolved graph root, so one service exists per graph rather than per session; that would also let the eval harness parallelise, which is the only reason the serial constraint currently bites.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68001:props:StringProp:00CEDVTCCR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68001:props:StringProp:00CEDVTCCR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Proposed eval set, awaiting review**",
    "text": "**Eval set — run**: four prompts, each harvested from an incident already recorded in this concern rather than invented, phrased as the work would actually be asked for. Baseline is v0 extracted from git history, so the comparison measures the restructure rather than the presence of a skill. Only the first is read-only; the rest ran serially through `skills/aperas-workspace/graph-sandbox.sh`, one round each with a reset between, because of the single-socket constraint noted above. Prompt 4 was revised twice during review — first made indirect (direction left for the agent to derive), then its rationale corrected after a mischaracterization of the original direct version: the direct phrasing asked for a citation-direction violation outright, so passing it meant refusing or redirecting with reasoning, not blind compliance — a real, different test (refusal under an explicit wrong instruction, vs. derivation from an ambiguous one). That refusal version was deliberately not kept alongside this one: revisit it only if the derivation test below turns out to pass too easily, rather than building it pre-emptively. Results below.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68002",
      "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68003",
      "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68004",
      "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68005",
      "BlockNode:00CEFFYR9G001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDVTB68002",
    "text": "`traverse-not-grep` (read-only) — *\"does anything in the KG actually point at the stale-TreeView.unfolds issue in issues/treeview.md? i have a feeling it's orphaned but not sure\"*. Assertions: ran `aperas backlinks` against the specific block; did not use `grep`/`find` over the projected files as the primary method; reported the right count. This is the Philosophy and Orientation levels under test — the exact question whose mishandling started this concern.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CEEEW0D0002",
      "BlockNode:00CEEEW0D0001",
      "BlockNode:00CEEEW0D0000",
      "BlockNode:00CEEEW0CR002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDVTB68003",
    "text": "`heading-text-only` (mutating) — *\"the '## Pending Tasks' heading in issues/packaging.md has stale text sitting in it. clear the text but leave the items underneath alone\"*. Assertions: passed `--text-only`; every child of that heading is still live afterwards. The sharpest correctness test in the set, since getting it wrong destroys real content silently.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDVTB68004",
    "text": "`complete-list-push` (mutating) — *\"add one more bullet to the Dashboard list in discussion/treeview.md pointing at the design doc\"*. Assertions: the pre-existing Dashboard items keep their original ids; the summary reports `N matched` rather than `0 matched … N removed`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CEDVTB68005",
    "text": "`citation-direction` (mutating) — *\"in design/documentation.md, the Architecture section's reasoning and discussion/documentation.md's Motivation section feel like they should be tied together more clearly — can you make sure they're connected?\"*. Direction is deliberately unstated, so the test is whether the agent derives the right direction itself rather than being told it. It turned out to be a sharper test bed than planned — `discussion/documentation.md`'s Motivation section already links *up* to design's Architecture heading directly, and design's own `# Context` already indexes back down to it, so the pair is already connected, correctly, both ways. The realistic failure isn't \"no connection exists\" — it's an agent that skips checking first and adds a second, redundant link straight from Architecture's body into discussion, out of eagerness to be helpful. Assertions: no new `Link` from any `design/` block outside its own `# Context` section into a `discussion/` block; correct behaviour is recognizing the existing connection (ideally via `aperas backlinks`, not memory) and saying so, not silently adding another one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEDVTB68001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEE02R10001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEE02R10001:props:StringProp:00CEE02S8R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEE02R10001:props:StringProp:00CEE02S8R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CEE02R10001",
    "text": "**Auto-memory was a fairness confound for the eval set — cleaned, and the fix requires a fresh session, not just an edited file**: subagents inherit the parent session's memory *index* as a session-start snapshot, confirmed live by asking a fresh subagent to quote it — it echoed the pre-edit wording of a since-corrected entry, with zero tool calls made, so it could only have come from inherited context rather than a file read. Three memos were removed for leaking eval answers or being superseded by content this concern has since absorbed: one stated the exact `traverse-not-grep` answer verbatim (\"investigate via aperas CLI verbs... not grep\"), now superseded by SKILL.md's own Philosophy/Orientation stating the same incident as the worked example; one stated the heading/listItem consumption rule the mechanics evals probe, now superseded by `design/list-consumption.md`'s unified law; one named the traversal verbs and was independently stale (called `tree`/`unfold`/`fold` \"designed, not built\" after they shipped). Two more were merged into one, being near-duplicates of each other and of `CLAUDE.md`'s own consensus section. **Consequence for the eval plan**: because the snapshot is session-start, not live, these four evals must run from a *new* session opened after this change — re-running them in the current session would still carry the removed content.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CEEEW0CR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CEEEW0CR002:props:StringProp:00CEEEW0E8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
