    "title": "00CF12B710001",
    "text": "**Three independent sightings of the same `reconcile.ts` identity-matching fragility, never tied together**: this doc's own [dead-container](../discussion/list-consumption.md#id/BlockNode:00CE5KSNVR002) and [tombstone-reuse](../discussion/list-consumption.md#id/BlockNode:00CE5KSNVR003) findings above, plus [packaging's `matchLeftoverByAbstract` position-sensitivity](../discussion/packaging.md#id/BlockNode:00CDEM7DVG002) (replaced by exact-key matching for renames, but never checked against `kg:update`'s own reconcile path), are three angles on one underlying defect family — `reconcile.ts`'s matching/lifecycle logic being unreliable around identity, not three unrelated bugs. None of the three write-ups reference either of the others. Worth one shared home once CRUD/reconciliation gets its own dedicated concern, per this doc's own note above.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE5KSNVR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1TZQVG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "holder": true,
    "title": "# CLI — Issues",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1TZQVG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "## Open Issues",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V65YG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "00CF1V65YG002",
    "text": "**A parent-heading list-push reconciles children by array position, tombstones included, not by content/key**: found running `aperas-skill`'s own eval against [issues/treeview.md's Open Issues list](../issues/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW638002) — piping the heading line plus every visible item (skipping tombstoned ones, as any reasonable read would) zips the piped items against storage slots by raw position, silently reassigning live content onto wrong ids once a tombstoned sibling is interleaved. Contradicts what both the skill and the code's own doc comments claim (\"exact-key matching\"). Root cause not yet read off `reconcile.ts` directly — identified from reproduced symptom, confirmed only in a disposable eval sandbox, not the live corpus. Same defect family as the next two items below — one fix in `reconcile.ts` likely closes all three.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V65YG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "00CF1V65YG003",
    "text": "**`aperas update`'s reconciliation can silently match fresh content onto an already-tombstoned node's id without reviving it**: reproduced twice in `list-consumption`'s eval sandbox — a new bullet's content lands on a stale, already-tombstoned id instead of a fresh one, and `tombstonedAt` is never cleared, so the content stays invisible even though the summary line reports a clean match/add. Root cause not yet investigated in `reconcile.ts`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V65YG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "**`matchLeftoverByAbstract` (Gestalt/Ratcliff-Obershelp recursion) is position-sensitive**",
    "text": "**`matchLeftoverByAbstract` (Gestalt/Ratcliff-Obershelp recursion) is position-sensitive**: works for reconciling siblings that share a rough common order, not for an unordered bag of whole-artifact identities scattered across concern folders — a match's recursive split can discard a whole quadrant of otherwise-valid candidates. Worked around for the rename path specifically (replaced by exact-key matching there), but the same algorithm is used elsewhere in `reconcile.ts` and may still carry the weakness on those other paths.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V65YG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "**A fully-dead container is never itself tombstoned**",
    "text": "**A fully-dead container is never itself tombstoned**: `pruneUnreachableTombstones` only collects nodes that are themselves tombstoned and unreachable; nothing ever tombstones a container once its last live child is gone (children get tombstoned one at a time as they're resolved, but the now-vacuous wrapper is never itself marked dead). Confirmed live against `issues/packaging.md`'s own dead nested-list-wrapper chain. Minor/cosmetic relative to the three above, but the same tombstone-lifecycle gap.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V65YG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "**A single `kg:insert` call isn't transactional**",
    "text": "**A single `kg:insert` call isn't transactional**: `kgInsert.ts`'s create mode resolves the parent, parses piped markdown into fresh nodes, checks name collisions, writes every new node into the store via `hydrateFromParsed`, resolves links, and only *then* resolves the `--after`/`--before` anchor — so an anchor-resolution failure leaves the newly-hydrated nodes live in memory, attached to nothing, with no rollback. `rejectSlugPathCollisions` is deliberately ordered ahead of hydration; this is a missing second guard (resolve/validate the anchor beside that same check, before any hydration), not a structural problem. `aperas reload -- --discard` is the only current recovery.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V65YR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "**A multi-artifact `kg:ingest` isn't transactional either**",
    "text": "**A multi-artifact `kg:ingest` isn't transactional either**: `ingestArtifacts`/`ingestAllArtifacts` (`apeironNgn/artifacts.ts`) loop per file with no try/catch, so a later file's failure leaves earlier files' writes already committed to the in-memory store with no rollback across the batch. Same shape as the single-`kg:insert` gap above, one layer up (a batch of artifacts vs. a batch of nodes within one call) — likely the same underlying fix (validate everything before writing anything) closes both.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V9JJ8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "## Sub-Parts",
    "text": "CLI's own known parts, and where each one's issues currently live:",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V9JJ8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "00CF1V9JJ8002",
    "text": "**`crud`** — not yet migrated; archived at `archive/Aperas-crud-design.md`, whose own §6 (\"Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this\") already names several items overlapping this doc's Open Issues above. Planned as `archive-migration`'s next sub-task.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V9JJ8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "**`treeview`** — 4 open",
    "text": "**`treeview`** — 4 open: stale `TreeView.unfolds` references, a bare `aperas unfold <ref>` mutating the default view instead of peeking, no raw single-node inspection command, and `unfold` not marking tombstoned children. See [Open Issues](../issues/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW638002).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1V9JJ8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-12T21:59:17.749Z",
    "title": "00CF1V9JJ8004",
    "text": "**`linking`** — none formally filed, but one confirmed-live bug not yet promoted: `extractAnchorNames`/`stripInlineAnchors` treat a backtick-quoted example anchor as a real claimed name, with no code-span awareness. See [discussion/aperas-skill.md](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CECCBKNG001). Formal home is [issues/linking.md](../issues/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV5A8002) — currently empty.",
