  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Open Questions",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10006",
      "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10006:props:StringProp:00CF1WZF6R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10006:props:StringProp:00CF1WZF6R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CF1WZE10006",
    "text": "Should `crud` (and the other sub-parts) ever become literal path-nested under a `core/` prefix, or stay flat top-level concerns cross-linked from here? The current Document Topology has no concept of concern nesting at all — introducing one would itself be a new design decision, not yet made.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF1WZE10007",
    "text": "Once `crud` is migrated (per `archive-migration`'s own plan), does its issues doc absorb the reconcile/tombstone bug family wholesale from here, leaving `cli`'s own Open Issues holding only the insert/ingest transactionality items and the Sub-Parts rollup — or does `cli` keep hosting the cross-cutting content permanently, with `crud` holding only what's specific to reconciliation as a data model? Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Settled",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18001",
      "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18002",
      "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18001:props:StringProp:00CF1WZF6R002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18001:props:StringProp:00CF1WZF6R003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Scope**",
    "text": "**Scope**: `core` is the shared ApeironNgn engine — `treeview`, `linking`, `list-consumption`, and `crud` (once migrated) are its sub-parts. `cli` and a future `webapp` are thin wrappers over that same engine, each getting their own separate, much thinner concern rather than being folded in here. This concern was originally scoped and named `cli` before that distinction was drawn; see the rename note below.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF1WZE18002",
    "text": "**The previous `cli.md` → `cli-packaging.md` → `packaging.md` rename is not evidence against this concern existing**: that doc was mis-scoped from the start — it was really packaging/distribution content wearing the \"cli\" name, not a case of \"cli\" being too broad a name to use. This time the scope is deliberate: the whole tool, not one facet of it. **Update**: this concern itself then turned out to be mis-scoped a second time — not because \"cli\" was too broad, but because the actual content (`reconcile.ts`, `node.ts`, `astParser.ts`, `apeironNgn/artifacts.ts`) is core engine code, not CLI-dispatcher code. Renamed to `core`; a genuinely thin `cli` was split back out separately. Two different failure modes wearing the same symptom (a rename), not the same mistake twice.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Sequencing**",
    "text": "**Sequencing**: `issues/core.md`, `discussion/core.md`, and `planning/core.md` (renamed from `cli.md`) hold the engine-level Open Issues and their fix plan; `design/core.md` and `history/core.md` stay deferred until the fixes actually settle the architecture and land. A separate, much thinner `cli` concern now holds only what's genuinely CLI-dispatcher-specific.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Freeflow",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE10001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18005",
      "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18006",
      "BlockNode:00CF282PR0001",
      "BlockNode:00CF2B9GGG001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18005:props:StringProp:00CF1WZF70000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18005:props:StringProp:00CF1WZF70001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Where this concern's Open Issues came from**",
    "text": "**Where this concern's Open Issues came from**: a whole-graph dense-linking pass surfaced the same underlying `reconcile.ts` defect family described independently in three separate places — `aperas-skill`'s own eval findings, `list-consumption`'s Checkpoints, and `packaging`'s discussion — plus two separate insert/ingest transactionality gaps (`aperas-skill`'s single-`kg:insert` finding, `linking`'s multi-artifact `kg:ingest` finding). None of the four originating write-ups referenced each other; compiling them here is what made the shared root cause visible in the first place.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Live incident while creating this very doc set**",
    "text": "**Live incident while creating this very doc set**: attempted the graph-first \"sketch a structure\" path (`aperas resolve --create-holder`) to scaffold `issues/cli.md` from nothing. Filled real content into the placeholder's Block children successfully, but `aperas project` then refused (\"still has a placeholder (holder) node somewhere in its subtree\"), and `kg:insert`'s promote path rejected the `ArtifactNode` outright (\"kg:insert only positions Block-level nodes\"). No verb exists to clear `.holder` on a Folder/Artifact once real content lands underneath it — confirmed by direct inspection, matches `Aperas-crud-design.md` §4.1/§6 exactly. Recovered by tombstoning the stuck holder chain (`aperas remove`) and redoing both `cli.md` docs disk-first instead — the same path `archive-migration`'s own established practice already uses for a genuinely new concern-doc-set. Filed as this concern's own Open Issues entry, since it's now a live-reproduced instance rather than just an archived design note.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
